    "answer": {
      "short": "For every positive integer $n$, the $n+1$ consecutive squares beginning with $[n(2n+1)]^2$ have the same sum as the next $n$ consecutive squares.\n\nExpand about the middle value $2n(n+1)$; the difference of the two sums is zero.",
      "worked": "Conjecture: for each positive integer $n$,\n$$\\sum_{j=0}^{n}[n(2n+1)+j]^2=\\sum_{j=n+1}^{2n}[n(2n+1)+j]^2.$$\nThe examples correspond to $n=1,2,3$.\n\nLet $M=2n(n+1)$. The left side is $\\sum_{k=0}^n(M-k)^2$ and the right side is $\\sum_{k=1}^n(M+k)^2$. Their difference $D$ is\n$$\\begin{align*}D&=M^2+\\sum_{k=1}^n\\bigl((M-k)^2-(M+k)^2\\bigr)\\\\D&=M^2-4M\\sum_{k=1}^n k\\\\D&=M^2-4M\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\\\D&=M\\bigl(M-2n(n+1)\\bigr)\\\\D&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore the two sums are equal, proving the conjecture.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
